test_mmap — `mmap` / `munmap` / `mprotect` end-to-end
=====================================================

Phase 1 chunk 4 + invariant-#1 close-out + master-roadmap demo
("`mmap`-based test program allocates 4 MB anon, writes pattern,
reads back"). Validates the userspace mmap surface (Linux ARM64
generic ABI numbers 222 / 215 / 226). All three are routed by the
shim layer (`oskit.posix.posix_dispatch`) into kernel-side
helpers; address allocation goes through the per-process VMA
tree via `vma_find_free_range`. The 4 MiB span crosses the
2-MiB user-PT boundary that Phase-1 chunk-4-bring-up was capped
to; both arches' `vm_create_process_pt` now allocate 8 leaf
tables (16 MiB) so this test exercises a second user PT/L3.

Steps:

1. `mmap(NULL, 4MB, RW, ANON|PRIVATE)` — VMA-tree-allocated; we
   only require the result to be page-aligned and inside the user
   VA window. The pages aren't faulted in until the first access.
2. Write a stride-pattern across the region (one i64 per 4 KiB
   page = 1024 page faults via `vma_handle_fault`, each installing
   a fresh zero frame).
3. Read the pattern back to confirm both the writes and the
   reads went through correctly.
4. `mprotect(p, 4MB, READ)` — strips PROT_WRITE. The PTE walk
   updates the AP bits on every faulted-in page. We don't try to
   trigger the resulting fault (no SIGSEGV delivery yet — that's
   Phase 2); we only verify the call returns 0 and a subsequent
   read still succeeds.
5. `munmap(p, 4MB)` — splits/removes the VMA and frees the
   physical frames back to the page pool.
6. **Reuse check.** A fresh `mmap` of the same size must land at
   the same base — the old bump-pointer would have advanced
   past it; first-fit `vma_find_free_range` reuses it. This is
   the invariant-#1-close-out regression test.

Pass criterion: prints `mmap: ok`.

    module oskit.bin.test_mmap

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with, syscall6}


    val SYS_MMAP     = 232
    val SYS_MUNMAP   = 249
    val SYS_MPROTECT = 234

    val PROT_READ  = 1
    val PROT_WRITE = 2

    val MAP_PRIVATE = 0x02
    val MAP_ANON    = 0x20

    val LEN: i64 = i64(0x400000)   // 4 MiB = 1024 pages


    print_idec(v: int)
        var n = v
        if n < 0
            puts("-")
            n = -n
        if n >= 10
            print_idec(n / 10)
        var b: [1]byte
        b[0] = byte('0' + (n % 10))
        puts(string(&b[0], 1))


    fail(code: int)
        puts("mmap: bad ")
        print_idec(code)
        puts("\n")
        exit_with(code)


    main(argv: []string)
        val rc = syscall6(SYS_MMAP, i64(0), LEN,
                          i64(PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE),
                          i64(MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANON),
                          i64(-1), i64(0))
        if rc <= i64(0)
            fail(1)
            return
        val base = u64(rc)

The address comes from the VMA tree's first-fit search — we
don't pin it to a specific number any more (that's what the
shim's chunk-4 bump pointer did). Just check it's page-aligned
and inside the user-VA window.

        if (base & u64(0xFFF)) != u64(0)
            fail(2)
            return
        if base < u64(0x60000000)
            fail(2)
            return
        if base + u64(LEN) > u64(0x61000000)
            fail(2)
            return

Stripe-write — touch every page in the region and store a
deterministic value derived from the offset. Each page faults
in fresh on first touch.

        var i: u64 = u64(0)
        while i < u64(LEN)
            var p: *i64 = *i64(base + i)
            *p = i64(0x12340000) + i64(i)
            i += u64(0x1000)

Read everything back and verify.

        i = u64(0)
        while i < u64(LEN)
            var p: *i64 = *i64(base + i)
            if *p != i64(0x12340000) + i64(i)
                fail(3)
                return
            i += u64(0x1000)

`mprotect` to read-only. We don't attempt a write to verify the
fault — that path requires SIGSEGV delivery (Phase 2 work). Just
confirm the syscall returns 0 and a read still works.

        val mpr = syscall6(SYS_MPROTECT, rc, LEN, i64(PROT_READ),
                           i64(0), i64(0), i64(0))
        if mpr != i64(0)
            fail(4)
            return
        var p0: *i64 = *i64(base)
        if *p0 != i64(0x12340000)
            fail(5)
            return

`munmap` releases the VMAs and frees the underlying frames. We
can't easily verify the frames went back to the pool from
userspace — the regression test is "boot the OS, run this binary
N times, observe no leak in the page-pool watermark." Chunk-4's
acceptance bar is just "mmap: ok".

        val um = syscall6(SYS_MUNMAP, rc, LEN, i64(0), i64(0),
                          i64(0), i64(0))
        if um != i64(0)
            fail(6)
            return

Step 6 — reuse. A fresh allocation of the same size should find
the just-freed gap and land at the same base. This is the
visible win over the legacy bump pointer.

        val rc2 = syscall6(SYS_MMAP, i64(0), LEN,
                           i64(PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE),
                           i64(MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANON),
                           i64(-1), i64(0))
        if rc2 <= i64(0)
            fail(7)
            return
        if u64(rc2) != base
            fail(8)
            return
        val um2 = syscall6(SYS_MUNMAP, rc2, LEN, i64(0), i64(0),
                           i64(0), i64(0))
        if um2 != i64(0)
            fail(9)
            return

        puts("mmap: ok\n")
        exit()
